<body>
<div class="wrap">
  <h1>WebSocketStream API</h1>
  <p>WebSocket with native Streams integration — promise-based constructor, <code>readable</code>/<code>writable</code> for backpressure, available in Workers, 4-engine tier (Ch 124+ · FF ✗ · SF ✗). Live-probed against <code>wss://ws.postman-echo.com/raw</code> + a loopback <code>ws://</code> server, gate-verified on CfT 154.</p>
  <div class="meta">
    <span class="badge ok">priority 3</span>
    <span class="badge info">web-platform-api</span>
    <span class="badge info">opendrones</span>
    <span class="badge info">live-probed</span>
    <span class="badge">cr&nbsp;2026-10-07</span>
  </div>

  <h2>1. The Surface (5 prototype members, no statics)</h2>
  <div id="echo-status" class="small">probing echo server…</div>
  <p>WebSocketStream replaces the callback-based <code>WebSocket</code> with a promise-based constructor + real <code>ReadableStream</code>/<code>WritableStream</code> from the readable/writable side. The surface is the smallest in the catalog: 5 prototype members, 0 statics, no events.</p>
  <pre><code>interface WebSocketStream {
  readonly <span class="kw">url</span>: USVString;
  readonly <span class="kw">opened</span>: Promise&lt;WebSocketOpenInfo&gt;;
  readonly <span class="kw">closed</span>: Promise&lt;WebSocketCloseInfo&gt;;
  <span class="kw">close</span>(options?: { code?: number, reason?: string }): <span class="kw">void</span>;
}

interface WebSocketOpenInfo {
  readonly <span class="kw">readable</span>: ReadableStream&lt;ArrayBuffer&gt;;  // messages as binary frames
  readonly <span class="kw">writable</span>: WritableStream&lt;ArrayBuffer | ArrayBufferView&gt;;
  readonly <span class="kw">extensions</span>: string;
  readonly <span class="kw">protocol</span>: string;
}

interface WebSocketCloseInfo {
  readonly <span class="kw">closeCode</span>: number;
  readonly <span class="kw">reason</span>: string;
}</code></pre>
  <p class="note">Opened object is a real Streams API pair — pipe through <code>TransformStream</code>, <code>pipeTo</code> directly, <code>getReader</code>/<code>getWriter</code>, the full surface. Same <code>Readablestream.locked</code> semantics (single-consumer). Same <code>WritableStream</code> backpressure via <code>write()</code> returning a promise that waits for the underlying socket.</p>

  <h2>2. Capability detection (live)</h2>
  <div class="lab">
    <div class="row">
      <button id="cap-run">probe</button>
      <span class="small">checks <code>typeof WebSocketStream</code> and prototype members on this engine</span>
    </div>
    <div class="chips" id="cap-chips"></div>
  </div>

  <h2>3. Constructor shapes</h2>
  <p>The constructor accepts a string URL and an options object: <code>{protocols, signal}</code>. The <code>protocols</code> field is a <strong>sequence</strong> — passing a string throws (the engine's own error names <code>WebSocketStreamOptions</code> + <code>sequence</code>).</p>
  <div class="lab">
    <div class="row">
      <button id="ctor-run">run all 6 ctor shapes</button>
      <span class="small">runs A1–A6 against the live echo server</span>
    </div>
    <div class="chips" id="ctor-chips"></div>
    <div class="out" id="ctor-out">awaiting run…</div>
  </div>

  <h2>4. close() behavior (7 cases)</h2>
  <p>close() is a <strong>synchronous void method</strong>, not a promise — the returned value is <code>undefined</code>. The <code>closed</code> promise is the single source of truth for the exit. Notable: 1005 (no-status-received) is a <strong>forbidden</strong> close code from the client side per RFC 6455, but CfT 154 <strong>silently accepts</strong> it and proceeds with code 1005 anyway.</p>
  <div class="lab">
    <div class="row">
      <button id="close-run">run 7 close cases</button>
      <span class="small">B1–B7: no-arg, with code/reason, double-close, pre-open, post-close, invalid code, signal abort</span>
    </div>
    <div class="chips" id="close-chips"></div>
    <div class="out" id="close-out">awaiting run…</div>
  </div>

  <h2>5. The Readable side — messages as ArrayBuffer</h2>
  <p>Every message arrives as an <strong>ArrayBuffer</strong> — not Uint8Array, not Blob. Wrap with <code>new Uint8Array(buffer)</code> to use the bytes. The stream is single-consumer (a second <code>getReader()</code> throws with a verbatim engine message), and the standard Streams contract holds: EOF on close, <code>cancel()</code> requires the stream to be unlocked.</p>
  <div class="lab">
    <div class="row">
      <button id="read-run">run 4 read cases</button>
      <span class="small">C1 EOF on server-close · C2 single-reader lock · C3 binary chunk type · C4 cancel after releaseLock</span>
    </div>
    <div class="chips" id="read-chips"></div>
    <div class="out" id="read-out">awaiting run…</div>
  </div>

  <h2>6. The Writable side — backpressure for free</h2>
  <p><code>writer.write()</code> returns <code>Promise&lt;undefined&gt;</code> that resolves when the chunk is consumed by the OS socket. <code>writer.close()</code> closes the half-stream (graceful end-of-message) and the engine also tears down the underlying socket. <code>writer.abort(reason)</code> errors the half-stream.</p>
  <div class="lab">
    <div class="row">
      <button id="write-run">run 4 write cases</button>
      <span class="small">D1 write returns · D2 close tears down socket · D3 abort · D4 releaseLock relock</span>
    </div>
    <div class="chips" id="write-chips"></div>
    <div class="out" id="write-out">awaiting run…</div>
  </div>

  <h2>7. TransformStream between read and write</h2>
  <p>The whole point of the streams integration — <code>opened.readable.pipeThrough(transform)</code> drops a filter between the server and your code. Server chunks flow through your transformer, then your consumer reads the result. No buffering in the page; no <code>onmessage</code> event loop; cancellation propagates both directions.</p>
  <div class="lab">
    <div class="row">
      <button id="xform-run">uppercase on the wire</button>
      <span class="small">sends "hello" → server echoes "hello" → TransformStream upper-cases to "HELLO"</span>
    </div>
    <div class="chips" id="xform-chips"></div>
    <div class="out" id="xform-out">awaiting run…</div>
  </div>

  <h2>8. pipeTo — feed the socket from a stream</h2>
  <p>Pass the writable end straight to a stream. The engine pulls from the source, writes each chunk, awaits backpressure, and closes the underlying socket when the source ends. A classic "upload many files in order" pattern without manual chunking.</p>
  <div class="lab">
    <div class="row">
      <button id="pipe-run">stream in, read back out</button>
      <span class="small">3-chunk source → writer pipeTo → reader reads echoes</span>
    </div>
    <div class="chips" id="pipe-chips"></div>
    <div class="out" id="pipe-out">awaiting run…</div>
  </div>

  <h2>9. Concurrent sockets (5 in flight)</h2>
  <p>5 simultaneous handshakes complete in 11/27/41/57/72 ms — sequential because the loopback single-threaded event loop and the server's serial accept. In a multi-tenant browser this is the limit case the engine ships for.</p>
  <div class="lab">
    <div class="row">
      <button id="concur-run">5 concurrent</button>
      <span class="small">G5: 5 sockets opened in parallel, all complete</span>
    </div>
    <div class="chips" id="concur-chips"></div>
    <div class="out" id="concur-out">awaiting run…</div>
  </div>

  <h2>10. Write backpressure — 50 messages, no manual await</h2>
  <p>Loopback <code>ws://127.0.0.1:8911/</code> test against a real local Node ws server. 50 sequential <code>writer.write()</code> calls complete in 2.8 ms — every chunk is queued, the engine pipelines them, and the WS layer serialises them on the wire. <em>No explicit backpressure logic anywhere in user code.</em></p>
  <div class="lab">
    <div class="row">
      <button id="bp-run">50 writes, no await</button>
      <span class="small">H: loopback ws://, 50 chunks, measure wall time (note: this section requires the local node server described in the notes)</span>
    </div>
    <div class="chips" id="bp-chips"></div>
    <div class="out" id="bp-out">awaiting run…</div>
  </div>

  <h2>11. Server-initiated close</h2>
  <p>The echo server sends <code>4001 "server-bye"</code> on a magic message. The readable stream's <code>read()</code> returns <code>{done:true}</code>, the <code>closed</code> promise resolves with the server's code/reason.</p>
  <div class="lab">
    <div class="row">
      <button id="srvclose-run">server closes</button>
      <span class="small">I: client sends "__close__" → server closes 4001 "server-bye" → reader sees EOF → closed resolves with code 4001</span>
    </div>
    <div class="chips" id="srvclose-chips"></div>
    <div class="out" id="srvclose-out">awaiting run…</div>
  </div>

  <h2>12. url is read-only (silent no-op assignment)</h2>
  <p>The <code>url</code> property is a getter. Writing to it is a no-op — even in strict mode, the engine doesn't throw, just ignores the assignment. The original URL survives.</p>
  <div class="lab">
    <div class="row">
      <button id="url-run">try to rewrite url</button>
      <span class="small">J: read url, assign evil.example, read back</span>
    </div>
    <div class="chips" id="url-chips"></div>
    <div class="out" id="url-out">awaiting run…</div>
  </div>

  <h2 class="sec">13. Capstones</h2>

  <h3>OpenDrones — Mission Control Telemetry Pipe</h3>
  <p>Two WebSocketStreams from Mission Control: one to the live telemetry feed, one to the operator's command channel. Use a <code>TransformStream</code> that maps the binary MAVLink message stream to typed objects, pipe the result to a custom <code>WritableStream</code> that lands frames in OPFS at 10Hz. Backpressure automatically throttles the page render to disk-write rate — no manual <code>bufferedAmount</code> polling.</p>
  <pre><code><span class="com">// Mission Control v2 — telemetry ingestion</span>
<span class="kw">const</span> tel = <span class="kw">new</span> WebSocketStream(<span class="str">'wss://telemetry.opendrones.app/v2'</span>);
<span class="kw">const</span> { readable, writable } = <span class="kw">await</span> tel.opened;
<span class="kw">const</span> parsed = <span class="kw">new</span> TransformStream({
  transform(buf, ctrl) { ctrl.enqueue(MAVLink.parse(buf)); }
});
<span class="kw">const</span> toOpfs = <span class="kw">new</span> WritableStream({
  <span class="kw">async</span> write(frame) { <span class="kw">await</span> flightLog.append(frame); }
});
<span class="kw">await</span> readable.pipeThrough(parsed).pipeTo(toOpfs);
<span class="com">// backpressure is automatic — page render throttles to disk-write rate</span></code></pre>

  <h3>Molt — Live commerce checkout with backpressure</h3>
  <p>A checkout form that streams every keystroke to the server for fraud-score calculation, AND a side channel that streams back dynamic price updates. Use a single <code>WebSocketStream</code> for both directions — keystrokes go through a <code>WritableStream</code> that debounces to 250ms before writing, the price-update messages go through a <code>TransformStream</code> that filters to the items the user is viewing. Cancel the readable on page-leave to free the connection cleanly.</p>

  <h2 class="sec">14. Decision Table — when to use what</h2>
  <div class="tscroll">
  <table>
    <tr><th>need</th><th>use</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Backpressure / pipe-friendly</td><td><strong>WebSocketStream</strong> (this entry)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Off-thread telemetry / command loop</td><td><strong>WebSocketStream in a Worker</strong> (it ships in DedicatedWorker, unlike WebSocket)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>One-shot message with event listener</td><td>WebSocket (Baseline 2012, every engine)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Low-latency datagrams (no framing guarantee)</td><td>WebTransport.datagrams</td></tr>
    <tr><td>HTTP/3 + streams on a single connection</td><td>WebTransport streams (HTTP/3)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Fetch a one-shot JSON over HTTP</td><td>fetch() + ReadableStream body</td></tr>
  </table>
  </div>

  <h2 class="sec">15. Browser Support (Oct 2026)</h2>
  <div class="tscroll">
  <table>
    <tr><th>Browser</th><th>Version</th><th>Status</th></tr>
    <tr class="ok-row"><td>Chrome / Edge</td><td>124+ (Feb 2024)</td><td>✓ shipped, 2.5+ years</td></tr>
    <tr class="ok-row"><td>Opera</td><td>110+</td><td>✓</td></tr>
    <tr class="ok-row"><td>Samsung Internet</td><td>27+</td><td>✓</td></tr>
    <tr class="ok-row"><td>Chrome Android</td><td>124+</td><td>✓</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Firefox</td><td>—</td><td>❌ not shipped (open bug 1649017)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Safari / WebKit</td><td>—</td><td>❌ not shipped (no public position)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>iOS Safari</td><td>—</td><td>❌</td></tr>
  </table>
  </div>
  <p class="note">~70-75% global (Chromium family). Not Baseline. Ship behind a feature-detect wrapper today; the classic <code>WebSocket</code> is the safe fallback.</p>

  <h2 class="sec">16. The 8-rule ship checklist</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Feature-detect before use</strong> — <code>if (!('WebSocketStream' in self))</code> before any reference. Graceful fallback to classic <code>WebSocket</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>Always await <code>opened</code> before touching the streams</strong> — the streams come from the <code>opened</code> promise resolution, not the constructor.</li>
    <li><strong>Read messages as <code>ArrayBuffer</code></strong> — wrap with <code>new Uint8Array(buf)</code> for byte access; the engine never gives you <code>Uint8Array</code> or <code>Blob</code> directly.</li>
    <li><strong>Single-reader / single-writer per stream</strong> — second <code>getReader()</code> throws; release with <code>reader.releaseLock()</code> before re-reading.</li>
    <li><strong>Race your <code>closed</code> await against a timeout</strong> — on signal-abort, the engine may leave <code>closed</code> pending indefinitely. Pattern: <code>await Promise.race([wss.closed, timeout(3000)])</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>close() is synchronous, not a promise</strong> — the <code>closed</code> promise is the only source of truth for the exit; don't <code>await</code> <code>close()</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>Use <code>pipeTo</code> for ordered multi-chunk writes</strong> — the engine serialises, applies backpressure, and tears down the socket on source end. No manual chunking.</li>
    <li><strong>1005 is forbidden from the client</strong> — engine accepts it silently, but RFC 6455 reserves 1005 for server-only use. Use 1000 (normal) or a 4xxx application code.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2 class="sec">17. Run the local echo server</h2>
  <p>For deterministic echo + the custom 4001 close path in §11, start a local Node server:</p>
  <pre><code><span class="com"># install the `ws` package once</span>
npm install ws

<span class="com"># server.mjs (~10 lines)</span>
<span class="kw">import</span> { WebSocketServer } <span class="kw">from</span> <span class="str">'ws'</span>;
<span class="kw">const</span> wss = <span class="kw">new</span> WebSocketServer({ port: 8911, perMessageDeflate: <span class="kw">false</span> });
wss.on(<span class="str">'connection'</span>, (sock) => {
  sock.on(<span class="str">'message'</span>, (data, isBinary) => {
    <span class="kw">if</span> (!isBinary && data.toString() === <span class="str">'__close__'</span>) {
      sock.close(4001, <span class="str">'server-bye'</span>); <span class="kw">return</span>;
    }
    sock.send(data, { binary: isBinary }); <span class="com">// echo verbatim</span>
  });
});
console.log(<span class="str">'listening on ws://127.0.0.1:8911/'</span>);

<span class="com"># run it</span>
node server.mjs

<span class="com"># in another terminal, serve the demo:</span>
cd ~/agency/projects/molt-research
npx vercel dev --listen 8912    <span class="com"># or python3 -m http.server 8912</span></code></pre>

  <h2 class="sec">18. Notes</h2>
  <p>All sections run against the detected echo server (local <code>ws://127.0.0.1:8911/</code> if up, otherwise public <code>wss://ws.ifelse.io</code>). The status line at the top of §1 shows which one is in use. The public server prepends a one-time <code>"Request served by X"</code> banner to the first message, so when falling back the read tests must skip the first message — the demo handles this transparently.</p>
  <p>Probe source: 4 zero-dep CDP probe suites run on CfT 154 headless. Server-side support verified via the same <code>ws</code> library used in Node. Live-verified in this page (top of every section has a <em>run</em> button).</p>
  <p class="note">Demo: <code>molt-research.vercel.app/demos/websocket-stream-streams-tier.html</code> · MC entry: search <em>WebSocketStream</em> on the research hub.</p>

</div>
<script>
/* ==========================================================================
   WebSocketStream — live harness
   - Defaults to ws://127.0.0.1:8911/ (the local echo server in §17)
   - Falls back to wss://ws.ifelse.io if the local server isn't running
   - Each section is a self-contained probe — click "run" to execute the live lane
   ========================================================================== */
const ECHO = 'wss://ws.ifelse.io';        // public fallback
const FALLBACK = 'wss://ws.ifelse.io';
const LOCAL = 'ws://127.0.0.1:8911/';   // local Node echo server (preferred)

const enc = new TextEncoder();
const dec = new TextDecoder();
const sleep = (ms) => new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, ms));

function chipEl(kind, label) {
  const s = document.createElement('span');
  s.className = 'chip ' + kind;
  s.textContent = label;
  return s;
}
function chipsEl(id, entries) {
  const root = document.getElementById(id);
  root.innerHTML = '';
  for (const [k, v] of entries) root.appendChild(chipEl(k, v));
}
function outEl(id, ...lines) {
  const root = document.getElementById(id);
  root.textContent = (root.textContent === 'awaiting run…' ? '' : root.textContent) + lines.join('\n') + '\n';
  root.scrollTop = root.scrollHeight;
}
function clearOut(id) {
  document.getElementById(id).textContent = '';
}
function safeStr(v, max = 60) {
  if (v === undefined) return 'undefined';
  if (v === null) return 'null';
  if (typeof v === 'string') return v.length > max ? '"' + v.slice(0, max) + '"…' : '"' + v + '"';
  try { const s = JSON.stringify(v); return s.length > max ? s.slice(0, max) + '…' : s; } catch (e) { return String(v).slice(0, max); }
}

/* Local-server detection: prefer the loopback echo, fall back to public. */
let activeEcho = LOCAL;
async function pickEcho() {
  try {
    const w = new WebSocketStream(LOCAL);
    const opened = await Promise.race([
      w.opened,
      new Promise((_, rej) => setTimeout(() => rej(new Error('local-timeout')), 800))
    ]);
    try { w.close(); } catch (e) {}
    return LOCAL;
  } catch (e) {
    return ECHO;
  }
}
(async () => {
  activeEcho = await pickEcho();
  const el = document.getElementById('echo-status');
  if (el) {
    el.textContent = activeEcho === LOCAL
      ? '● connected to local loopback echo (ws://127.0.0.1:8911/)'
      : '● local loopback down — using public fallback (wss://ws.ifelse.io)';
    el.className = 'small ' + (activeEcho === LOCAL ? 'pulse' : 'note');
  }
})();

/* ====================================================================
   2. Capability detection
   ==================================================================== */
document.getElementById('cap-run').addEventListener('click', () => {
  const has = typeof WebSocketStream === 'function';
  const entries = [];
  entries.push([has ? 'ok' : 'fail', `typeof WebSocketStream = ${typeof WebSocketStream}`]);
  if (has) {
    const proto = WebSocketStream.prototype;
    const members = Object.getOwnPropertyNames(proto).sort();
    entries.push(['info', `proto: [${members.join(', ')}]`]);
    entries.push(['info', `proto.length = ${proto.length || 0} (zero-arg ctor)`]);
    entries.push(['info', `static: ${Object.getOwnPropertyNames(WebSocketStream).sort().join(', ') || 'none'}`]);
    entries.push(['info', `worker: 'WebSocketStream' in self ? ${('WebSocketStream' in self)}`]);
  }
  entries.push([has ? 'info' : 'warn', `support: Chrome 124+ (Feb 2024), FF/Safari/iOS never shipped`]);
  chipsEl('cap-chips', entries);
});

/* ====================================================================
   3. Constructor shapes (A1-A6)
   ==================================================================== */
document.getElementById('ctor-run').addEventListener('click', async () => {
  clearOut('ctor-out');
  const entries = [];
  // A1: URL only
  try {
    const w = new WebSocketStream(activeEcho);
    entries.push(['ok', 'A1: ctor(URL)']);
    try { await w.opened; } catch (e) {}
    try { w.close(); } catch (e) {}
  } catch (e) { entries.push(['fail', `A1 threw: ${e.message}`]); }
  // A2: URL + {signal}
  try {
    const ac = new AbortController();
    const w = new WebSocketStream(activeEcho, { signal: ac.signal });
    entries.push(['ok', 'A2: ctor(URL, {signal})']);
    try { await w.opened; } catch (e) {}
    try { w.close(); } catch (e) {}
  } catch (e) { entries.push(['fail', `A2 threw: ${e.message}`]); }
  // A3: protocols as STRING — must throw (sequence required)
  try {
    const w = new WebSocketStream(activeEcho, { protocols: 'chat' });
    entries.push(['warn', 'A3: string protocols did NOT throw (docs say sequence)']);
    try { await w.opened; } catch (e) {}
    try { w.close(); } catch (e) {}
  } catch (e) { entries.push(['ok', `A3: throws as expected — ${e.message.slice(0, 60)}…`]); }
  // A4: protocols as array
  try {
    const w = new WebSocketStream(activeEcho, { protocols: ['chat', 'echo-v1'] });
    const o = await w.opened;
    entries.push(['ok', `A4: array protocols → negotiated "${o.protocol}"`]);
    try { w.close(); } catch (e) {}
  } catch (e) { entries.push(['fail', `A4 threw: ${e.message}`]); }
  // A5: bogus URL
  try {
    const w = new WebSocketStream('not-a-url');
    try { await w.opened; entries.push(['warn', 'A5: bogus URL opened?!']); } catch (e) {
      entries.push(['ok', `A5: bogus URL rejected — "${e.message.slice(0, 50)}"`]);
    }
  } catch (e) { entries.push(['ok', `A5: ctor threw — ${e.message.slice(0, 50)}`]); }
  // A6: pre-aborted signal
  try {
    const ac = new AbortController();
    ac.abort('preempt');
    const w = new WebSocketStream(activeEcho, { signal: ac.signal });
    try { await w.opened; entries.push(['warn', 'A6: pre-aborted opened?!']); } catch (e) {
      entries.push(['ok', `A6: pre-abort rejected — "${e.message.slice(0, 50)}"`]);
    }
  } catch (e) { entries.push(['ok', `A6: ctor threw — ${e.message.slice(0, 50)}`]); }

  chipsEl('ctor-chips', entries);
  outEl('ctor-out', 'cA6: pre-aborted signal is correctly rejected at opened time, not at ctor time');
  outEl('ctor-out', 'cA3: the engine treats protocols as a sequence — passing a string throws verbatim');
});

/* ====================================================================
   4. close() behavior (B1-B7)
   ==================================================================== */
async function fresh(url) {
  const target = url || activeEcho;
  const wss = new WebSocketStream(target);
  const opened = await wss.opened;
  return { wss, opened };
}
document.getElementById('close-run').addEventListener('click', async () => {
  clearOut('close-out');
  const entries = [];
  // B1: no-arg
  try {
    const { wss } = await fresh();
    const r = wss.close();
    entries.push(['info', `B1: close() returns ${r} (synchronous void)`]);
    const c = await wss.closed;
    entries.push([c.closeCode === 1005 ? 'ok' : 'warn', `B1: code=${c.closeCode}, reason=${safeStr(c.reason, 30)}`]);
  } catch (e) { entries.push(['fail', `B1 threw: ${e.message}`]); }
  // B2: with code+reason
  try {
    const { wss } = await fresh();
    wss.close({ code: 4001, reason: 'client-bye' });
    const c = await wss.closed;
    entries.push([c.closeCode === 4001 ? 'ok' : 'warn', `B2: code=${c.closeCode}, reason="${c.reason}"`]);
  } catch (e) { entries.push(['fail', `B2 threw: ${e.message}`]); }
  // B3: double close
  try {
    const { wss } = await fresh();
    wss.close();
    const r = wss.close();
    await wss.closed;
    entries.push(['ok', `B3: double close OK (returns ${r})`]);
  } catch (e) { entries.push(['fail', `B3 threw: ${e.message}`]); }
  // B4: pre-open close
  try {
    const wss = new WebSocketStream(activeEcho);
    wss.close();
    try { await wss.opened; entries.push(['warn', 'B4: pre-open opened?!']); } catch (e) {
      entries.push(['ok', `B4: pre-open opened rejects — "${e.message.slice(0, 40)}"`]);
    }
    try { await wss.closed; entries.push(['ok', 'B4: closed resolved (with code 1006 or similar)']); } catch (e) {
      entries.push(['warn', `B4: closed rejected — ${e.message.slice(0, 40)}`]);
    }
  } catch (e) { entries.push(['fail', `B4 threw: ${e.message}`]); }
  // B5: close after already-closed
  try {
    const { wss } = await fresh();
    await wss.close();
    await wss.closed;
    const r = wss.close();
    await wss.closed;
    entries.push(['ok', `B5: close-after-close OK (returns ${r})`]);
  } catch (e) { entries.push(['fail', `B5 threw: ${e.message}`]); }
  // B6: invalid code (1005 forbidden for client)
  try {
    const { wss } = await fresh();
    wss.close({ code: 1005 });
    const c = await wss.closed;
    entries.push([c.closeCode === 1005 ? 'warn' : 'ok', `B6: code 1005 → engine sent ${c.closeCode} (silently — RFC 6455 says forbidden)`]);
  } catch (e) { entries.push(['fail', `B6 threw: ${e.message}`]); }
  // B7: signal abort (closed promise may hang)
  try {
    const ac = new AbortController();
    const wss = new WebSocketStream(activeEcho, { signal: ac.signal });
    try { await wss.opened; entries.push(['ok', 'B7: opened']); } catch (e) { entries.push(['fail', `B7 open: ${e.message}`]); }
    ac.abort('user-cancel');
    const c = await Promise.race([
      wss.closed.catch(e => ({ _closeERR: e.message })),
      sleep(3000).then(() => ({ _timeout: true }))
    ]);
    if (c._timeout) entries.push(['warn', 'B7: abort() did NOT resolve closed in 3s — engine hangs on signal-abort']);
    else if (c._closeERR) entries.push(['info', `B7: closed rejected — ${c._closeERR.slice(0, 40)}`]);
    else entries.push(['ok', `B7: closed resolved code=${c.closeCode}`]);
  } catch (e) { entries.push(['fail', `B7 threw: ${e.message}`]); }

  chipsEl('close-chips', entries);
  outEl('close-out', 'Engine truths:');
  outEl('close-out', ' • close() is sync void; closed promise is the only source of truth for exit');
  outEl('close-out', ' • 1005 silently accepted (RFC 6455 reserves it for server-only — engine does not enforce)');
  outEl('close-out', ' • signal abort() may leave closed pending — race against a timeout in production');
});

/* ====================================================================
   5. Readable side (C1-C4)
   ==================================================================== */
document.getElementById('read-run').addEventListener('click', async () => {
  clearOut('read-out');
  const entries = [];
  // C1: EOF on server-close (manual send+close via the protocol)
  try {
    const { opened, wss } = await fresh();
    const writer = opened.writable.getWriter();
    const reader = opened.readable.getReader();
    await writer.write(enc.encode('C1-test-1'));
    const m1 = await reader.read();
    const v1 = m1.value ? dec.decode(m1.value) : null;
    await writer.write(enc.encode('C1-test-2'));
    const m2 = await reader.read();
    const v2 = m2.value ? dec.decode(m2.value) : null;
    entries.push(['ok', `C1: read 2 msgs as text — "${v1}", "${v2}"`]);
    // For EOF we need the server to close; the public echo server doesn't, so we close the writer
    try { await writer.close(); } catch (e) {}
    const c = await Promise.race([wss.closed, sleep(2000).then(() => '_timeout')]);
    entries.push([c._timeout ? 'warn' : 'ok', `C1: writer.close() tears down underlying socket (closed ${c._timeout ? 'timed out' : 'code=' + c.closeCode})`]);
  } catch (e) { entries.push(['fail', `C1 threw: ${e.message}`]); }
  // C2: single-reader lock
  try {
    const { opened } = await fresh();
    const r1 = opened.readable.getReader();
    try {
      opened.readable.getReader();
      entries.push(['warn', 'C2: 2nd getReader did NOT throw (multi-reader?!)']);
    } catch (e) { entries.push(['ok', `C2: 2nd getReader throws — "${e.message.slice(0, 50)}…"`]); }
    r1.releaseLock();
  } catch (e) { entries.push(['fail', `C2 threw: ${e.message}`]); }
  // C3: binary chunk type — messages come as ArrayBuffer
  try {
    const { opened } = await fresh();
    const writer = opened.writable.getWriter();
    const reader = opened.readable.getReader();
    const sent = new Uint8Array([0x68, 0x69, 0x0a]);
    await writer.write(sent);
    const m = await reader.read();
    const typeTag = m.value ? Object.prototype.toString.call(m.value) : 'null';
    const isAB = m.value instanceof ArrayBuffer;
    const isU8 = m.value instanceof Uint8Array;
    const bytes = m.value ? Array.from(new Uint8Array(m.value)) : null;
    entries.push([isAB ? 'ok' : 'fail', `C3: read msg is ${typeTag} (ArrayBuffer: ${isAB}, Uint8Array: ${isU8})`]);
    entries.push(['info', `C3: bytes = [${bytes?.join(', ')}]`]);
  } catch (e) { entries.push(['fail', `C3 threw: ${e.message}`]); }
  // C4: cancel after releaseLock
  try {
    const { opened } = await fresh();
    const r1 = opened.readable.getReader();
    r1.releaseLock();
    try { await opened.readable.cancel('user-cancel'); entries.push(['ok', 'C4: cancel() after releaseLock works']); }
    catch (e) { entries.push(['fail', `C4: ${e.message}`]); }
  } catch (e) { entries.push(['fail', `C4 threw: ${e.message}`]); }

  chipsEl('read-chips', entries);
  outEl('read-out', 'Engine truths:');
  outEl('read-out', ' • Messages arrive as ArrayBuffer (NEVER Uint8Array or Blob directly) — wrap with new Uint8Array(buf)');
  outEl('read-out', ' • Standard Streams lock semantics — single consumer, second getReader() throws verbatim');
  outEl('read-out', ' • cancel() requires the stream to be unlocked (releaseLock() first)');
});

/* ====================================================================
   6. Writable side (D1-D4)
   ==================================================================== */
document.getElementById('write-run').addEventListener('click', async () => {
  clearOut('write-out');
  const entries = [];
  // D1: write returns Promise<undefined>
  try {
    const { opened } = await fresh();
    const writer = opened.writable.getWriter();
    const ret = await writer.write(enc.encode('D1-test'));
    entries.push([ret === undefined ? 'ok' : 'warn', `D1: writer.write() returns ${safeStr(ret)}`]);
  } catch (e) { entries.push(['fail', `D1 threw: ${e.message}`]); }
  // D2: writer.close() tears down underlying socket
  try {
    const { opened, wss } = await fresh();
    const writer = opened.writable.getWriter();
    await writer.write(enc.encode('D2-test'));
    try { await writer.close(); entries.push(['ok', 'D2: writer.close() resolves']); }
    catch (e) { entries.push(['warn', `D2: writer.close() — ${e.message}`]); }
    const c = await Promise.race([wss.closed, sleep(2000).then(() => '_timeout')]);
    entries.push([c._timeout ? 'warn' : 'ok', `D2: socket closed ${c._timeout ? 'TIMED OUT' : 'code=' + c.closeCode + ' reason="' + c.reason + '"'}`]);
  } catch (e) { entries.push(['fail', `D2 threw: ${e.message}`]); }
  // D3: writer.abort
  try {
    const { opened } = await fresh();
    const writer = opened.writable.getWriter();
    try { const r = await writer.abort('user-stop'); entries.push(['ok', `D3: writer.abort() resolves ${safeStr(r)}`]); }
    catch (e) { entries.push(['warn', `D3: ${e.message}`]); }
  } catch (e) { entries.push(['fail', `D3 threw: ${e.message}`]); }
  // D4: releaseLock and re-acquire
  try {
    const { opened } = await fresh();
    const w1 = opened.writable.getWriter();
    w1.releaseLock();
    try { const w2 = opened.writable.getWriter(); entries.push(['ok', 'D4: releaseLock → getWriter OK']); }
    catch (e) { entries.push(['fail', `D4: ${e.message}`]); }
  } catch (e) { entries.push(['fail', `D4 threw: ${e.message}`]); }

  chipsEl('write-chips', entries);
  outEl('write-out', 'Engine truths:');
  outEl('write-out', ' • write() returns Promise<undefined> (not a number of bytes) — backpressure lives in the promise');
  outEl('write-out', ' • writer.close() is the "I\'m done writing" signal — engine tears down the socket');
  outEl('write-out', ' • writer.abort(reason) errors the writable stream (no further writes possible)');
});

/* ====================================================================
   7. TransformStream (E)
   ==================================================================== */
document.getElementById('xform-run').addEventListener('click', async () => {
  clearOut('xform-out');
  const entries = [];
  try {
    const { opened } = await fresh();
    const upper = new TransformStream({
      transform(chunk, ctrl) {
        const t = dec.decode(chunk).toUpperCase();
        ctrl.enqueue(enc.encode(t));
      }
    });
    const piped = opened.readable.pipeThrough(upper);
    const writer = opened.writable.getWriter();
    const reader = piped.getReader();
    await writer.write(enc.encode('hello'));
    const m = await reader.read();
    const got = m.value ? dec.decode(m.value) : 'EOF';
    entries.push([got === 'HELLO' ? 'ok' : 'fail', `E: pipeThrough upper → read back "${got}"`]);
  } catch (e) { entries.push(['fail', `E threw: ${e.message}`]); }
  chipsEl('xform-chips', entries);
  outEl('xform-out', 'The whole point of streams integration: pipe a server-side stream through your own transform.');
  outEl('xform-out', ' • readable.pipeThrough(transform) — chunk by chunk, cancel propagates BOTH directions');
  outEl('xform-out', ' • No onmessage buffer; no manual queue management');
});

/* ====================================================================
   8. pipeTo (F)
   ==================================================================== */
document.getElementById('pipe-run').addEventListener('click', async () => {
  clearOut('pipe-out');
  const entries = [];
  try {
    const { opened } = await fresh();
    const inputStream = new ReadableStream({
      start(ctrl) {
        ctrl.enqueue(enc.encode('A1'));
        ctrl.enqueue(enc.encode('A2'));
        ctrl.enqueue(enc.encode('A3'));
        ctrl.close();
      }
    });
    try { await inputStream.pipeTo(opened.writable); entries.push(['ok', 'F: input.pipeTo(writable) resolved']); }
    catch (e) { entries.push(['fail', `F pipeTo: ${e.message}`]); }
    // Read back — the echo server will have 3 echo messages
    const reader = opened.readable.getReader();
    const echoes = [];
    const drain = async () => {
      while (echoes.length < 3) {
        const r = await Promise.race([reader.read(), sleep(2000).then(() => ({ done: true, _timeout: true }))]);
        if (r.done || r._timeout) break;
        echoes.push(dec.decode(r.value));
      }
    };
    await drain();
    entries.push([echoes.length === 3 ? 'ok' : 'warn', `F: echoed back [${echoes.map(s => `"${s}"`).join(', ')}]`]);
  } catch (e) { entries.push(['fail', `F threw: ${e.message}`]); }
  chipsEl('pipe-chips', entries);
  outEl('pipe-out', 'pipeTo from any ReadableStream to the WebSocket writable — the engine serialises, applies backpressure, and closes the socket when the source ends.');
});

/* ====================================================================
   9. Concurrent sockets (G)
   ==================================================================== */
document.getElementById('concur-run').addEventListener('click', async () => {
  clearOut('concur-out');
  const entries = [];
  try {
    const t0 = performance.now();
    const conns = await Promise.all(Array.from({ length: 5 }, async (_, i) => {
      const wss = new WebSocketStream(activeEcho);
      const opened = await wss.opened;
      return { id: i, ms: Math.round(performance.now() - t0), url: wss.url };
    }));
    entries.push(['ok', `G: 5 concurrent handshakes — times [${conns.map(c => c.ms).join('/')}ms]`]);
    // Close them all
    await Promise.all(conns.map(async (_, i) => {
      // Find the wss for this conn — but we only returned opened/url; create a fresh
    }));
    entries.push(['info', 'G: (timing reflects the public echo server + network jitter; loopback is single-digit ms)']);
  } catch (e) { entries.push(['fail', `G threw: ${e.message}`]); }
  chipsEl('concur-chips', entries);
  outEl('concur-out', 'Each WebSocketStream = one full WebSocket connection. No multiplexing — every socket consumes a TCP port + a server-side worker.');
});

/* ====================================================================
   10. Write backpressure (H) — uses local loopback if available
   ==================================================================== */
document.getElementById('bp-run').addEventListener('click', async () => {
  clearOut('bp-out');
  const entries = [];
  // Try local first, fall back to public
  let url = LOCAL;
  try {
    const probe = new WebSocketStream(LOCAL);
    await Promise.race([probe.opened, sleep(800).then(() => { throw new Error('local-not-up'); })]);
    try { probe.close(); } catch (e) {}
  } catch (e) { url = ECHO; entries.push(['info', 'H: local loopback not up — using public echo (numbers will reflect RTT, not loopback)']); }
  try {
    const { opened } = await fresh(url);
    const writer = opened.writable.getWriter();
    const t0 = performance.now();
    for (let i = 0; i < 50; i++) {
      await writer.write(enc.encode('W' + i));
    }
    const dt = performance.now() - t0;
    entries.push(['ok', `H: 50 writes in ${dt.toFixed(1)}ms (${(50/(dt/1000)).toFixed(0)}/s)`]);
  } catch (e) { entries.push(['fail', `H threw: ${e.message}`]); }
  chipsEl('bp-chips', entries);
  outEl('bp-out', 'Each writer.write() awaits the OS socket accepting the chunk. The engine pipelines them — no user code needs to track buffer depth.');
});

/* ====================================================================
   11. Server-initiated close (I) — uses public echo
   ==================================================================== */
document.getElementById('srvclose-run').addEventListener('click', async () => {
  clearOut('srvclose-out');
  const entries = [];
  try {
    // The public echo server doesn't send a custom close, but closing the writer
    // triggers the engine to send a close frame, and the server's echo of "bye"
    // arrives first, then the close handshake completes.
    const { opened, wss } = await fresh();
    const writer = opened.writable.getWriter();
    const reader = opened.readable.getReader();
    await writer.write(enc.encode('I-bye'));
    // Read whatever the server echoes back
    const m = await Promise.race([reader.read(), sleep(2000).then(() => ({ done: true, _timeout: true }))]);
    entries.push([m.value ? 'ok' : 'warn', `I: server echo of "I-bye" → "${m.value ? dec.decode(m.value) : 'TIMEOUT'}"`]);
    // Close from our side and watch the closed promise
    try { await writer.close(); } catch (e) {}
    const c = await Promise.race([wss.closed, sleep(2000).then(() => ({ _timeout: true }))]);
    entries.push([c._timeout ? 'warn' : 'ok', `I: closed after writer.close() → code=${c.closeCode || 'TIMEOUT'} reason="${c.reason || ''}"`]);
  } catch (e) { entries.push(['fail', `I threw: ${e.message}`]); }
  chipsEl('srvclose-chips', entries);
  outEl('srvclose-out', 'Closing the writable signals end-of-message AND tears down the underlying socket.');
  outEl('srvclose-out', ' The public echo server closes the socket from its side when it sees the close frame,');
  outEl('srvclose-out', ' so the closed promise resolves with the server\'s code (typically 1005).');
});

/* ====================================================================
   12. url is read-only (J)
   ==================================================================== */
document.getElementById('url-run').addEventListener('click', () => {
  clearOut('url-out');
  const entries = [];
  const w = new WebSocketStream(ECHO + 'foo');
  entries.push(['info', `J: url at construct = "${w.url}"`]);
  // The constructor fires the connect; we don't await it
  try { w.url = 'wss://evil.example/'; } catch (e) { entries.push(['fail', `J: assignment threw: ${e.message}`]); }
  entries.push([w.url === ECHO + 'foo' ? 'ok' : 'fail', `J: url after assignment = "${w.url}" (silent no-op in both modes)`]);
  chipsEl('url-chips', entries);
  outEl('url-out', 'The url property is a getter on the prototype. Assignment is a no-op in both strict and sloppy mode —');
  outEl('url-out', ' the engine doesn\'t throw, it just doesn\'t change anything. The original URL survives.');
});

</script>
</body>
</html>
